Sval: Enter on an open-tab, pin or command row goes through Sval

A row of Sval's open tabs (`SvalLauncherTabs`) may be a tab of a workspace
no window shows, which has no Firefox tab for `switchToTabHavingURI` to
find. Picking it calls the panel's own select, which changes workspace in
the one place a window does and materialises the tab as a click on its row
does (`SvalPanel.goToTab`). Firefox turns a modified Enter on a switch-to-tab
row into a load; on Sval's row every Enter switches, so a modifier never
duplicates an open tab. The empty tab the row was picked in closes once the
switch has happened, as Firefox closes it after a switch to a tab. Alt+Enter
on it splits this tab with it instead. So the address bar sets no
`action-override` while a modifier is held: Firefox sets it so the next
Enter loads a switch-to-tab row's page in place, and its row reads as a
page to visit, while in the address bar every Enter on such a row switches
or splits.

Enter on a pin's row (`SvalFavoritesProvider`) shows the pin's page through
the pins row's own select, as a click on the pin does, where Firefox would
load the pin's address in the tab the field was typed in; Enter on a
Favorite's row opens it as the Favorites menu does (`SvalFavorites.open`).

Where Enter opens a page is Sval's mapping (`SvalLauncherEnter.whereFor`):
Shift for a new tab, Accel+Shift for a new window and Alt for a split, where
Firefox uses Shift for a window and Alt for a tab. `_whereToOpen` is where
every pick and every Enter asks, and it is told the row picked, which
Firefox does not pass. Firefox turns a keyed new tab back into this tab when
this tab is empty; Sval's Shift+Enter always opens a new tab, so Home stays
where it was. A split is no place `openTrustedLinkIn` knows, and a
pin is no tab Firefox restricts (`allowPinnedTabHostChange` reads
`tab.pinned`), so `_loadURL`, which every page load from the field goes
through, asks Sval for both.

The address bar lists its rows in sections, each with a limit of its own
after Firefox's own count of rows (`SvalLauncherSections.maxResults`), so a
query asks for that many rows; the view shows no more rows than the query
asked for.

A command that picks something (`SvalLauncherCommands`) keeps the view open
on its choices, so its row is picked before `pickResult` closes the view,
and while the choices show, a query asks only Sval's commands provider,
through the query context's `providers`, which no input caller sets.

